# Autonomous AI Software Engineering Harness

Hackathon project for an autonomous coding harness that accepts a repository and a natural-language objective, understands the codebase, plans work, edits code, verifies the result, recovers from failures, and returns evidence.

## Product documents

- [Product Requirements Document](docs/PRD.md)
- [4-person execution plan](docs/TEAM_PLAN.md)
- [Parent and sub-issue breakdown](docs/ISSUE_BREAKDOWN.md)
- [Contribution rules](CONTRIBUTING.md)
- [Implementation backlog](https://github.com/affan80/pentester-AI-harness/issues)

## Core product flow

```text
Repository + Objective
        ↓
Intent / Goal Analysis
        ↓
Repository Intelligence
        ↓
Context Assembly
        ↓
Planning
        ↓
Execution + Tools
        ↓
Verification
   ┌────┴────┐
 PASS       FAIL
   │          │
 Audit     Recovery
   │          │
   └────┬─────┘
        ↓
 Evidence-backed result
```

The terminal interface accepts a project folder or HTTPS Git repository link,
then offers three workflows: bug/pipeline fix, end-to-end build, and autonomous
testing. The objective tells the engine what to fix or build.

## Run everything with one command

Requires Python 3.12+ and Make; Git is required for repository links. `make` runs
setup, compilation, repository hygiene, lint, all tests, the three demo scenarios,
and a non-interactive TUI test run. It does not wait for menu input.

```bash
make
```

Each stage is also available as a standalone target:

- `make setup` — create `.venv` and install the project, pytest, and Ruff
- `make build` — compile harness, TUI, demos, and scripts
- `make hygiene` — repository hygiene checks (`./scripts/ci.sh`)
- `make lint` — `ruff check .`
- `make test` — the pytest suite
- `make demo` — demo scenarios A/B/C with evidence reports under `runs/demo/`
- `make check` — hygiene, lint, and all tests
- `make tui` or `make run` — open the interactive project/workflow menu
- `make tui-smoke` — run the fixture's actual tests through the TUI
- `make clean` — remove run artifacts and caches
- `make help` — list all targets

## Start an interactive session

```bash
make setup
make tui
```

```text
Project folder or HTTPS repository link
> /path/to/project
Choose mode
  1. Bug and pipeline fix
  2. End-to-end build
  3. Autonomous end-to-end testing
> 3
Objective (Enter to run detected tests)
>
```

| Mode | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `fix` | Use the model to plan a bug/pipeline repair, apply scoped patches, verify, and retry within budget |
| `build` | Use the model to plan and implement the requested feature, including new files, then verify |
| `test` | Detect Python/Node checks, run the existing suite and E2E command, and save results without model-driven source edits |

Testing automatically uses a Node `test:e2e` or `e2e` script when present. Python
E2E tests run as part of the full pytest suite. Use `--test-command` for an
additional command. Target project dependencies and required services must be
installed/running. Missing tests never produce a verified result. Testing does
not generate a new browser/test framework or repair failures; use build/fix for
those changes. A model audit is not performed by the integrated runner.

HTTPS URLs are cloned to a fresh retained folder under `runs/checkouts/`; local
folders are used directly. Fix/build can change files in that folder. Use
`--scope` to restrict file edits. Repository links must not contain credentials;
use Git's configured authentication for private repositories.

Scripted examples (after setup):

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m tui.cli ./my-project --mode test --non-interactive
.venv/bin/python -m tui.cli ./my-project --mode test --test-command "npm run test:e2e"
.venv/bin/python -m tui.cli https://github.com/owner/repo.git --mode test
.venv/bin/python -m tui.cli ./my-project "Fix the failing CI pipeline" --mode fix
.venv/bin/python -m tui.cli ./my-project "Build a login page with tests" --mode build
```

Fix/build require a configured live model with structured output support. For
the existing OpenAI-compatible adapter, export configuration in your shell:

```bash
export HARNESS_MODEL_PROVIDER=openai
export HARNESS_MODEL="your-model-id"
# Set HARNESS_OPENAI_API_KEY using your local secret manager/shell environment.
# Optionally set HARNESS_MODEL_BASE_URL for an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.
make tui
```

See `.env.example` for the configuration names. `.env` files are not loaded
automatically. The default fake client is for scripted tests, so live fix/build
fail with an actionable message until configured. No credentials are needed for
`make demo`, `make tui-smoke`, or testing mode.

Every session saves `session.json`, `events.jsonl`, and `final-report.md` under
`runs/`; executed verification also saves `verification.json` and command
artifacts. Exit codes: `0` verified, `1` partial/inconclusive, `2` failed,
`3` cancelled, `64` invalid input. For backward compatibility, positional
repository/objective calls without `--mode` retain the inspection-only workflow.

The patch generator currently accepts source files up to 12,000 characters;
larger targets fail explicitly instead of receiving incomplete source context.

## CI/CD

Pull requests and pushes to `main` run `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. The workflow validates repository hygiene and automatically runs Python and Node checks when those project files appear.

Tags matching `v*` run `.github/workflows/release.yml`, re-run the repository checks, create a source archive and SHA-256 checksum, and upload them as release artifacts. A deployment target is intentionally not assumed until the runtime destination is chosen.

Run the same checks locally with:

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
./scripts/ci.sh
```

Missing pytest/Ruff fails the checks. Generated ignored files (including `.venv`
and `runs`) are excluded from the repository file-size check.
